  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler air arrives Monday, with scattered rain \nshowers, then rain and snow showers possible through Tuesday morning.\n\nOur streak of pleasant weather will come to an end tonight as a cold \nfront bears down upon our region. Moisture begins to wane along the \ncold front, and becomes confined to a narrow band, with what rain \nthat falls late tonight and tomorrow morning along the front likely \nto be light and within just a few hour period. \n\nColder air behind the front will introduce lake clouds and some \nprecipitation east of the Lakes. This strong cold air advection, \nwith 850 hPa temperatures lowering to -5 to -10C at 850 hPa by \nMonday night will create instability for lake showers of rain or \nsnow east of the Lakes, and especially over the higher terrain. \n\nThough the instability will be plenty, moisture behind the front \nwill be lacking, thus much of the lake response will be light, with \nthe best chances for measurable precipitation Monday night when a an \nuptick in ambient moisture within the mid level trough axis occurs.\n \nAll precipitation should end by late Tuesday morning with increasing \namounts of sunshine through the day. \n\nThe cold air advection Monday and Monday night will bring a brisk \nnorthwest flow to our region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures persist throughout the week, \nwith increasing chances for cool rain arriving Thursday.\n\nA deep longwave trough will develop across the lower Mississippi \nValley mid-week, with phasing shortwaves within the jet stream \nproducing a negatively tilted mid level trough over the southern \nAppalachians by Thursday. To the lee of the Appalachians and near \nthe Carolina coastline a surface low will develop, with a protruding \n1.5 PV anomaly down to near 650 hPa deepening a surface low as \nit tracks northward along the Atlantic Coastline.  \n\nAhead of this trough deeper Gulf moisture will surge northward, with \na widespread area of rain developing within height falls ahead of \nthe mid level trough, reaching our southern zones Thursday, and \nspreading northward through the afternoon and overnight hours. Model \nconsensus is increasing for precipitation probabilities with this \nsystem, with one-half of the ensemble members among the GFS, ECMWF \nand Canadian models depicting at least a half inch of rainfall with \nthis system. The lack of deep cold air will allow for much of the \nprecipitation to remain plain rain, with minor higher terrain snow \npossible later Thursday night and into Friday morning. \n\nA likely deformation band with this system will continue light \nshowers of rain and higher terrain snow showers through Friday and \ninto Friday night.  \n\nStay tuned for how this system develops and tracks heading into the \nholiday weekend.",
